all right there are a lot of questions  surrounding hospice care i spoke to a  southwest medical doctor to talk  facts and myths about the service check  it out we're breaking down the facts and  myths of hospice care now first and  foremost let's  define hospice for people who don't know  sure  so hospice is a level of care that's  provided for individuals  who have life-limiting illness and  either the illness can't be treated  anymore that the treatments are failing  or a person says  i don't want to go through these  treatments anymore so the chemotherapy  for their cancer is making them sicker  than  the cancer would and they say you know  what i want comfort care  i don't want to spend my time in  doctor's offices or pharmacies i want  the care to come to me  and uh medicare and medicaid and  insurances  cover that well sure debunking i guess  you could say  yeah the first question here is is  hospice actually a place  people have to go no in fact about 98  of our patients receive care in the home  hospice used to be a place for people to  go  but now the care goes to patients  wherever they live  so whether it's an assisted living or  their own home or an apartment  group home we take  the medical care the medications the  equipment the patient needs for comfort  and safety  all to them is hospice care expensive  so hospice care is covered almost all of  the time  by medicare or medicaid and private  insurances also  cover hospice care so there is a lot of  expense  in providing hospice care but it's not  paid by the patient  how long can someone be in hospice care  if someone  is living at six months and at every  certification opportunity  after that and they still meet the  criteria  or eligibility criteria for hospice they  stay in hospice care  so people sometimes think there's a time  limit or an expiration date  on the hospice services or the patient's  life and that's not true  right and is hospice care just for  cancer patients  no okay so we actuall